Original commit body from 8738b209d9f4e06d67b068f8baa66bc0cd99d33d:

torsocks sends every SOCKS5 message through
`static ssize_t (*send_data)(...) = send_data_impl;` then
`send_data(fd, buf, n)`. The call was emitted as the unresolved name
send_data, so the call graph stopped at the caller and taint never
reached the send() inside send_data_impl: a secret sent that way read
CONFIRMED.

c.py now collects each function-pointer variable the file initialises
(file scope or local) or assigns (`p = f` / `p = &f`), and a call
through it emits a `calls` edge to every function it holds
(evidence_type function_pointer, score 1/N for N held names). A held
name is resolved as a direct call to it would be, WI-rimon's rule
included: a name an included header declares is libc's even when the
repository defines one, and gets the unresolved libc edge. A held
name that is no function (a parameter, `*slot`) names nothing; a
pointer holding only such names, or never assigned (a parameter),
keeps its unresolved edge.

Measured against dev: torsocks 12 calls resolved (every send_data /
recv_data -> send_data_impl / recv_data_impl), redis 20 (zmalloc's OOM
handler, jemalloc's junk callbacks, pthread_create_fptr), crun 0, tmate
0; each replaces the unresolved edge, nothing else changes. The
fixture's getenv -> send_data(...) secret now reads violated.

INV-nohob's other two mechanisms are separate PRs: the DBG macro chain
(function-like macros wrapping log_print) and the global struct a
callee stores the secret into. Until the latter lands, torsocks'
host-secret-no-network still reads clean.

